  "journal": {
    "entry_id": "717bbc6d3f1964ad2603c6f8ac8a8c0c103da890e200314fe83c7d5dc2e42633",
    "text": "I read through the minbpe code to get oriented before changing anything. That covered minbpe/basic.py, minbpe/regex.py, minbpe/base.py, minbpe/gpt4.py, minbpe/__init__.py and the lineage_bench.py harness. I also ran one search for \"import\", which returned 60 matching lines, to map how the modules depend on each other.\n\nNothing was measured. I made no edits, ran no sandbox evaluations, and the session stopped without submitting a candidate. So I have no results to report.\n\nWhat I believe now is only structural. base.py holds the shared tokenizer machinery. basic.py and regex.py build the trainers on top of it, and gpt4.py is a thin wrapper. I have no evidence yet about where time is spent under the bench, so any claim about hot spots would be a guess.\n\nNext session I should start by running lineage_bench.py unmodified to get a baseline. Then I should profile the merge and encode loops in basic.py and regex.py, pick one concrete change, and evaluate it in the sandbox before submitting. I should not spend another session only reading.",
